# From ReLU Gates to Tropical Landscapes

A ReLU network and a tropical-rational pair look different, but they evaluate to the same piecewise-affine function. Begin with the central identity and change its two inputs yourself.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

The gate identity turns `(P,Q)` into `(max(P,Q),Q)`. Notice that the second component does not change.

<details><summary>Reveal the two-case proof</summary>

If $P\ge Q$, then both sides equal $P-Q$. If $P\le Q$, then both sides equal $0$. These cases establish $\max(P-Q,0)=\max(P,Q)-Q$.

</details>

## Negative weights are pair exchanges

The only tension between neural arithmetic and generalized tropical polynomials is that neural weights may be negative while internal tropical scaling is nonnegative. The next laboratory shows the resolution.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:0}}

<details><summary>Prerequisite: what is a generalized tropical polynomial?</summary>

It begins with affine forms $a\cdot x+b$ and is closed under ordinary sum, pointwise maximum, and multiplication by a nonnegative scalar. Such functions are convex and piecewise affine. A tropical rational function is a difference $P-Q$ of two of them. For background, see [tropical geometry](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tropical_geometry), [rectifiers](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rectifier_(neural_networks)), and [piecewise-linear functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Piecewise_linear_function).

</details>

## See the exact pair in motion

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

The two bends occur at $x=-2$ and $x=1$. Between breakpoints, both components and their difference are affine.

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the representation theorem and proof architecture</summary>

**Tropical–Neural Representation Theorem.** Every finite scalar feedforward ReLU network on $\mathbb R^n$ equals $P-Q$ for generalized max-plus tropical polynomials $P$ and $Q$, at every input.

The proof follows the network tree. Affine leaves become $(A,0)$. Pair addition models neural addition. A positive coefficient scales both components; a negative coefficient scales and exchanges them. A ReLU uses $(P,Q)\mapsto(\max(P,Q),Q)$. Structural induction proves equality at every node and therefore at the output.

</details>

## Depth, geometry, and nested gates

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

For two input coordinates, switching loci become lines and higher-dimensional polyhedral walls rather than isolated breakpoints.

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:1}}

<details><summary>Complexity and representation growth</summary>

Compilation is linear in the size of the retained expression graph, but unfolding shared neural nodes may duplicate work. Expanding every maximum into explicit affine terms can also grow rapidly. The exact theorem does not assert that the raw pair is minimal or canonical; dominated-term removal and cancellation are additional problems.

</details>

## Audit the equality numerically

{{demo:0}}

{{algorithm:2}}

Finite grids are excellent illustrations and regression tests, but they are not the source of the all-input equality. That equality follows from the algebraic identities at each constructor. Continue with resources on [convex functions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convex_function) and [polyhedral complexes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polyhedral_complex) to explore the geometry behind the pair.

